  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 214 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nTuesday onwards will see much of the western CONUS back under the \ninfluence of an upper-level ridge, with southwesterly upper-level \nflow expected across the CWA. This southwesterly flow will promote a \nfetch of Pacific moisture continuing to advect into the region \nthrough at least Thursday. As a result, despite the upper-level \nridge in place, daily precipitation chances will continue, \nespecially with multiple lobes of 500mb vorticity advecting across \nthe region each afternoon. After a cooler day Tuesday with highs in \nthe low- to mid-70s, temperatures will climb back into the 70s and \n80s across the region under the influence of upper-level ridging. \nSaturday is currently forecast to be the warmest day this week, but \nthis may change over the next 6 days. Other than daily precipitation \nchances, strong winds will be possible over the Panhandle Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning as a stout low-level jet develops \nacross the region. The GFS currently suggests a 45 to 50kt low-level \njet across the Panhandle late Tuesday night into early Wednesday \nmorning, favoring gusty winds across the Panhandle. GFS Omega field \nremain weak, so significant downward acceleration leading to high \nwinds is currently not anticipated. However, this setup will need to \nbe monitored in the event this jet strengthens or downward Omega \nvalues start to increase.",
